---
description: Never leave chrome or render-prop slots the library does not call
globs: **/*.ts,**/*.tsx
alwaysApply: true
---

# No dead chrome

**Incident:** `SectionReviewChrome.renderAppendix` (demo 👉 yellow box)
stayed on the review chrome bag after the only call site — unanswered
**fallback** shells — was removed. Hosts still had to implement a slot
the library never invoked. It was **not** the review remark UI
(`renderComment` is). Same class of leftover: `renderActionIcon("edit")`,
`tCommon("add")`, overlay `FormItemAddition` / `onSubmitFormItem`
(follow-ups commit via `renderAddFollowUp` / `renderFormItemsEditor`).

A chrome / render-prop / overlay-arg / union member exists **only** if
library code calls it. Not “mirrors fill”, not “host might want it”,
not “fallback just in case”.

When you delete a call site, **in the same change** delete:

1. the field (or union member) on the library type
2. every host/demo implementation of that slot
3. args that existed only to feed that slot

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — type requires a slot the walk never calls
type Chrome = { renderAppendix: (comment: string) => ReactNode; … };

// ✅ GOOD — every key has a library call; deleting the call deletes the key
```

Identity host wrappers (`({ items }) => items`) are fine **when the
library still calls them**. Unused args a host ignores (`setEntries`)
are not dead chrome; an entire unused function on the bag is.

Do **not** keep a second UI for a path the host already owns.

Related: `simplify-then-split.mdc`, `no-optional-defaults.mdc`.
